   "drive": "Where a car may be (brief 023). The site carries a drive grid (sim/data/handover.site.json `drive`, the walk grid's window and cells, made by tools/sim/drive.ts): a cell is drivable when its centre lies on a carriageway (within half a plan road's width of its centreline), in a kerb bay of the civic block, or on a drive (the world's vehicle crossing from a carriageway over a dropped kerb and the pavement to a bay or yard: civic-block drives[], Splice; the Handover's one is service-drive, Apron Road to serviceBay). Pavements, forecourts, yards and squares are not drivable. Every car that moves on the street routes on drivable cells only, by the walk grid's A* (8 neighbours, no corner cutting, ties by cell index, string-pulled on drivable cells): the turned driver's drive to the service bay with Vale in the back (by the carriageway and the drive, never across a pavement; brief 059), the getaway car's Drive between its stands (cell.driveCar), and the rival car between its points (rulebook rivals). A car's place that lies off the drive grid stands at its nearest drivable cell within 6 m (ties: lowest cell); a place with none, or a car's place that no drive reaches from the pickup, rejects the site at load (unknown = reject). A car keeps off the kerb (brief 023, Keel with Pulse): on a carriageway its centre keeps the site's drive.lane (half the city's lane, life.json traffic.laneWidth) from the edge of the drivable ground where it can, on a drive or in a bay it keeps to the middle; the route never runs nearer an edge than its grid cells did (deterministic, sim Grid.CarRoute). Timing is car.pace's: the drive to the service bay runs at its surfaces' pace from Vale-in (car.pickup), then waits parked in the bay (the driver's camera block is retired, brief 059). Parked in the service bay the car faces the site's car.serviceFacing (the bay's long axis, nose away from the drive it came by). The getaway car leaves the street at Go along its exit lane (getaway.exit, held to the carriageway by SX-1); the rival car drives in, drops three, parks, follows, leaves on a tip and comes back (rulebook rivals). Cars never collide with cars (no rule reads it).",
